Consider the C function foo and the binary tree shown.
typedef struct node {
int val;
struct node *left, *right;
} node;
int foo(node *p) {
int retval;
if (p == NULL)
return 0;
else {
retval = p->val + foo(p->left) + foo(p->right);
printf("%d ", retval);
return retval;
}
}
10
5
3
8
11
13
When foo is called with a pointer to the root node of the given binary tree, what
will it print?

questions.question_text_latex:
Consider the C function `foo` and the binary tree shown:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=C]
typedef struct node {
    int val;
    struct node *left, *right;
} node;
int foo(node *p) {
    int retval;
    if (p == NULL)
        return 0;
    else {
        retval = p->val + foo(p->left) + foo(p->right);
        printf("%d ", retval);
        return retval;
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}
In the binary tree, root is 10; its left child is 5 with children 3 and 8; its right child is 11 with right child 13.
When `foo` is called with a pointer to the root node of the given binary tree, what will it print?

